WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:05.549
Now that we've covered how our AI can sense the world around them, we can discuss how

2
00:00:05.549 --> 00:00:09.580
we can tailor the environment to give them objects to interact with.

3
00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:14.460
This is a new system added in Unreal Engine 5 called the smart object system.

4
00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.719
So what are smart objects?

5
00:00:16.719 --> 00:00:20.339
As the name suggests, they're objects placed in the world that both AI agents

6
00:00:20.339 --> 00:00:22.929
and players can interact with.

7
00:00:22.929 --> 00:00:26.550
These objects contain all of the necessary information to handle the interaction logic

8
00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:29.260
for that object.

9
00:00:29.260 --> 00:00:34.960
Smart objects are part of a global database of smart objects and they use partitioning logic.

10
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:41.049
This means that they can be queried at runtime using things such as location as an example.

11
00:00:41.049 --> 00:00:46.060
One of the key advantages of this system is that it also uses a reservation system.

12
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:51.890
This means that other AI agents or players can be aware of in-use smart objects near them.

13
00:00:51.890 --> 00:00:57.450
This prevents multiple AI agents selecting the same object at the same time.

14
00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:02.840
Smart objects have their own subsystem that keeps track of all of the smart objects in your level.

15
00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:07.799
This subsystem is created automatically in the level when the smart object plugin is enabled.

16
00:01:07.799 --> 00:01:12.439
It's worth noting that only one smart object subsystem can exist at any one time.

17
00:01:12.439 --> 00:01:17.890
However, it is worth noting that in multiplayer games, the smart object subsystem is only

18
00:01:17.890 --> 00:01:21.900
instantiated on the server or authority machine.

19
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:25.579
As we can see in the picture at the bottom of this slide, the smart object subsystem

20
00:01:25.579 --> 00:01:29.459
is used to query for and return smart objects.

21
00:01:29.459 --> 00:01:33.260
It's essentially a cache for our AI to bounce off of.

22
00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:37.780
The smart object collection contains a list of all smart object components associated

23
00:01:37.780 --> 00:01:39.859
within the level.

24
00:01:39.859 --> 00:01:44.620
The collection holds all shared configurations and is used to create all runtime instances

25
00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:50.299
of smart objects to enable efficient, large world simulation outside of loaded areas.

26
00:01:50.299 --> 00:01:55.319
And again, there is only one smart object collection per level.

27
00:01:55.319 --> 00:01:59.359
So now we know what smart objects are and how the backend system works, but how do

28
00:01:59.359 --> 00:02:02.500
we go about creating a smart object of our own?

29
00:02:02.500 --> 00:02:05.400
Well that's where the smart object component comes in.

30
00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:09.280
Smart object components can be added to any actor, thus converting them to a smart

31
00:02:09.280 --> 00:02:13.919
object, and these will be picked up in the smart object subsystem.

32
00:02:13.919 --> 00:02:18.990
This component sets up and stores the configuration of a given smart object template.

33
00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:22.949
This component still adheres to the world partitioning logic, so an actor holding this

34
00:02:22.949 --> 00:02:27.469
component can still be loaded and unloaded, whether using the world partitioning or level

35
00:02:27.469 --> 00:02:29.569
streaming systems.

36
00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:33.889
If the actor holding this component is spawned at runtime, then it will not remain in memory

37
00:02:33.889 --> 00:02:36.810
once it has been unloaded.

38
00:02:36.810 --> 00:02:41.250
A smart object definition is a data structure that contains immutable data that is shared

39
00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:44.620
between multiple instances of a smart object.

40
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:49.219
This definition stores filtering information such as user-required tags, descriptive tags

41
00:02:49.219 --> 00:02:52.789
and the default set of behaviors to execute on interaction.

42
00:02:52.789 --> 00:02:56.689
It also exposes out one or more available slots that can be used by either AI agents

43
00:02:56.689 --> 00:02:58.719
or players.

44
00:02:58.719 --> 00:03:01.259
Each of these slots contain the following.

45
00:03:01.259 --> 00:03:05.719
Firstly we have the location and rotation of the slot relative to the parent anchor,

46
00:03:05.719 --> 00:03:09.360
both of which are baked in from editor placement.

47
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:14.080
They also allow us to define a specific behavior per slot that can override the default

48
00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:16.969
set of behaviors for this object.

49
00:03:16.969 --> 00:03:23.039
They also allow us to define a set of gameplay tags, user tags and runtime tags.

50
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:26.520
Lastly we come to smart object behavior definitions.

51
00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:32.340
These definitions contain the data needed by an AI agent or player for a given interaction.

52
00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.139
Some common behavior examples are gameplay behaviors.

53
00:03:35.139 --> 00:03:39.280
These are used by a pawn or actor to perform their behaviors.

54
00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.819
There's player behaviors and this behavior is specifically built for player characters

55
00:03:42.819 --> 00:03:45.270
that can use smart objects.

56
00:03:45.270 --> 00:03:48.949
Or you could use the mass entity behaviors, which is a behavior that makes use of the

57
00:03:48.949 --> 00:03:54.189
smart object behavior definition to configure a smart object to be used by mass entities

58
00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:58.830
rather than usual gameplay behavior actor pawn architectures.

59
00:03:58.830 --> 00:04:03.389
Finally it's worth noting that gameplay behaviors can be implemented directly in Blueprint.
